## Two Paths: If-Else

`if` alone handles "do this when true." Add `else` for "otherwise, do this instead."

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let score = 85;

  if score >= 60 {
    io.println("You passed!");
  } else {
    io.println("You need to retake the test.");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

### How If-Else Works

```xiom
if condition {
  // Path A: condition is true
} else {
  // Path B: condition is false
}
// Both paths rejoin here
```

Exactly ONE of the two blocks runs — never both, never neither.

### Practical Examples

```xiom
// Check age
let age = 16;
if age >= 18 {
  io.println("You can vote.");
} else {
  io.println("You're too young to vote.");
}

// Check even/odd
let number = 7;
if number % 2 == 0 {
  io.println("Even");
} else {
  io.println("Odd");
}

// Validation
let password = "secret";
if password == "open_sesame" {
  io.println("Access granted!");
} else {
  io.println("Access denied.");
}
```

### If vs If-Else

```xiom
// Two independent ifs — BOTH could run
if score > 90 { io.println("Excellent!"); }
if score > 60 { io.println("Passed!"); }

// If-else — exactly ONE runs
if score >= 60 {
  io.println("Passed!");
} else {
  io.println("Failed.");
}
```

Use `if-else` when the two cases are mutually exclusive — one or the other, not both.

